WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:02.370
Standby.

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:04.890
Is the Nicholl Committee ready?

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.650
Great, letting them in now.

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:08.640
Connected.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:09.690
There she is.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.014
Hello, Jane.

00:00:11.014 --> 00:00:11.514
Hi, Kate.

00:00:11.514 --> 00:00:12.480
Hi, Kate.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.200
Hello, Beth.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.940
Hi Vanar, how are you?

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.510
I'm well, how are you?

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:17.340
Oh my!

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:18.930
So many people.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.930
I'm Jennifer Yuh Nelson, I'm the
chair of the Nicholl Fellowship

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:22.850
Committee.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:25.230
And I'm here with other members
of the Nicholl Committee

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:26.580
who read your script.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.380
You have won a
Nicholl Fellowship.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:32.148
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:32.148 --> 00:00:36.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.300
Oh my gosh.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.506
Wow.

00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:42.370
OK, I'm going to stop
having a panic attack now.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:43.860
Thank you.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.850
Gosh, thank you so much.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:48.670
This is the best news
I've ever received.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:52.550
Sorry, it feels like I just
got kicked in the brain.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:53.960
Whoa, I have Bounty.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:57.610
I have paper towels
in my armpits.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:00.250
I can't even describe it.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:01.360
It doesn't feel real.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.580
It feels very
validating to compete

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.470
with some great writers.

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:07.390
Thank you for recognizing
"Sins of My Father."

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:08.860
That is the biggest thing.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:13.270
This is such a meaningful
thing coming from the Academy

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:14.995
and from all of you.

00:01:14.995 --> 00:01:16.370
You just keep
writing and writing

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.730
and then one day you're heard.

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:20.500
I want to thank
you for hearing me.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.330
Thank you for your
work and good luck.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:25.390
Have a great rest of
your day and stay safe.

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:27.496
Wear those masks!

00:01:27.496 --> 00:01:28.400
Wow.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.210
Uh...

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:31.340
oh crap, I'm still on.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:32.620
I'm going to go.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:33.269
Bye!

00:01:33.269 --> 00:01:35.225
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:35.225 --> 00:01:38.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:39.422
Welcome everyone.

00:01:39.422 --> 00:01:41.880
I'm David Rubin, President of
the Academy of Motion Picture

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.650
Arts and Sciences.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.450
As a casting director,
of course I'm

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.900
thrilled by uncovering
exciting new acting talent.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.450
But I think what excites me
and my fellow Academy members

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:55.860
most is the discovery of new
stories and new storytellers.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.870
The mission of the Academy's
Nicholl Fellowships

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.810
in Screenwriting is to
lift up new storytellers

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:02.970
and to champion their talent.

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:06.870
Today, that mission
resonates more than ever.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.180
Now in its 35th year,
the Nicholl competition

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.950
received a record 7,831
submissions from across the US

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:18.450
and over 70 countries, including
248 colleges and universities.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:22.110
Their stories were read by 79
professional script readers,

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.510
including those trained in
our diverse reader training

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:25.710
workshop.

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:29.070
Next, 207 members of the
Academy winnowed down

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:31.500
this multifaceted collection.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.350
And then our incomparable
Nicholl Fellowships Committee,

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:36.240
chaired by Short Films
and Feature Animation

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:39.540
governor, Jennifer Yuh Nelson,
and including Writers Branch

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.170
Governor, Larry Karaszewski, and
former Academy President, John

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.150
Bailey, made the
final selections.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:48.370
In the end, the committee
embraced five winning stories.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:50.850
It is these five screenplays
and the writers behind them

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:52.620
that we celebrate now.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:58.140
Vanar Jaddou, Kate Marks,
James Acker, Jane Therese,

00:02:58.140 --> 00:02:59.820
and Beth Curry.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:01.860
To begin our program,
I'm excited to introduce

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.290
our host, Aldis Hodge.

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:07.440
His remarkable acting talent
has lit up screens big and small

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.390
in a broad ranging body of work.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:11.740
Watch for him soon in
the role of Jim Brown,

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:13.530
in Academy Award
winner Regina King's

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:16.570
directorial debut
"One Night in Miami."

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:20.230
Aldis, the Nicholl
story is in your hands.

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:21.100
Take it away.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.200
Thank you, David.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.350
Welcome everyone to the
2020 Nicholl Fellowships

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:27.810
in Screenwriting
Awards and Table Read.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.840
Now as an actor, a
host, and an advocate,

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.440
I'm blessed with the opportunity
to help tell amazing stories.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:37.830
And today, I'm joined by an
incredible collection of talent

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:40.350
as we celebrate five new
storytellers and the Nicholl

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:42.400
Fellowship winning screenplays.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.060
The directors of our show,
Burlee and Abel Vang,

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:48.060
are the writing, directing
team that won the 2011 Nicholl

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.980
Fellowship for their
screenplay, "The Tiger's Child."

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:52.380
They just wrapped
post-production

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:55.320
on their most recent feature
film, "They Live in the Gray."

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:58.460
Our fellow actors are
Lou Diamond Phillips.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:01.620
Lou's trailblazing body of
work as an actor, director,

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:04.440
and author includes
performances as Don Lucho

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.990
in "The 33," about the
Chilean miners trapped

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:09.750
for 69 days in 2010.

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:13.620
Stephanie Beatriz currently
stars as Detective Rosa Diaz

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.529
on NBC's Golden Globe
award-winning comedy,

00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:18.300
"Brooklyn Nine-Nine."

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:19.470
Michael Pena.

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:22.980
A Chicagoan and first generation
Mexican-American, Michael

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:24.860
has enthralled
audiences in turns

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:27.150
such as Mike Zavala
in "End of Watch"

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:31.050
and as Kiki Camarena in
Netflix's "Narcos" franchise.

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:33.420
Rounding out our cast
is Taylor Russell.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:36.480
Taylor starred as Emily in
the breakout film "Waves"

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:39.780
for which she was honored with
the Virtuoso Award at the 2020

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.390
Santa Barbara International
Film Festival.

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:44.280
Through the power
of their stories,

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:48.330
the five new Nicholl Fellows,
Vanar, James, Jane, Kate,

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:50.860
and Beth helped
bring us together.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:52.090
Let's meet them.

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:55.240
James Acker is a
2020 Nicholl Fellow.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:58.030
His winning screenplay
is "SadBoi."

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:01.150
The script chronicles the
trials of an emotionally stunted

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:04.510
highschooler who, after being
thrown out of his house,

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:06.490
must revisit old
friends and repair

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:09.670
burnt bridges while searching
for a place to sleep.

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:12.310
James grew up in New
Jersey and Tennessee.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:14.560
He's compelled to write
coming of age stories

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:16.060
from different perspectives.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:18.730
He powerfully notes that
stories like "SadBoi"

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:21.370
weren't really being told
when he was growing up.

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:23.740
So he penned his
Nicholl winning effort

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:25.780
to explore some of
the harder lessons

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:30.550
that so many queer youth have to
learn at an all too early age.

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:33.880
Here's James in his own words.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:37.270
When I was in the fifth
grade, I was in trouble a lot.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:40.180
But the one thing I could
never mess up was writing.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:43.690
For me, I just love
writing in the YA pocket.

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:47.320
I really like talking about
what kids go through, especially

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:48.670
through the high school years.

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:51.880
They're allowed to feel
everything all the time.

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:56.590
And from that, I got to
"SadBoi" just because it's

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:59.880
sort of an amalgamation
of all of these things

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:00.880
since high school.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:03.190
I kept a lot inside back then.

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:06.190
And when trying to dig
deep, a lot of those things

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:07.040
come back out.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:10.120
And I feel like oftentimes,
especially in youth stories

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:12.010
with queer characters,
a lot of the time it's

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:14.025
rooted in some severe abuse.

00:06:14.025 --> 00:06:15.400
And it was really
important to me

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:19.180
to show just a nice,
innocent summer romance.

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:21.520
In this scene from
my script "SadBoi,"

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:24.250
an emotionally stunted
runaway, Edgar Bisset,

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.140
dreams of a quiet
afternoon with his best

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:28.660
friend Peter, his
only confidant,

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:30.420
before everything in
his life fell apart.

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:36.300
Exterior, the duplex evening.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:38.590
Edgar, 16, sits on his porch.

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:41.840
He ices his eye with a
bag of frozen cauliflower.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:44.780
Peter sits with him, holding
the yellow backpack in his lap.

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:46.490
They wait in silence.

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:48.650
A man and a woman scream
at each other somewhere

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:50.330
inside the duplex.

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:52.900
Peter looks at Edgar,
there's blood behind his bag.

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:59.310
I think you need more
than cauliflower.

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:01.500
Edgar stares at the
front lawn, blank.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:06.850
Eddie, you need to see a doctor.

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:08.860
Edgar fiddles with a pebble.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:11.350
The screaming inside increases.

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:15.280
Peter looks at the duplex
and shakes his head.

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:17.530
You didn't deserve that.

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:20.920
We don't gotta talk about it.

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:22.680
We should though.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:25.465
Well, I don't want to.

00:07:25.465 --> 00:07:27.620
But we talk about
everything, Eddie.

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:29.533
I don't want to talk
about everything.

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:35.580
OK.

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:38.650
I ask you something you
don't want to talk about.

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:40.680
"Cauliflower."

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.125
Edgar thinks on it, then nods.

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:47.930
Have you told anyone?

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:50.130
No.

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:53.315
Is it always an alcohol thing?

00:07:53.315 --> 00:07:53.815
Always.

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:57.860
Does it happen a lot?

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:03.630
Why?

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:11.386
Cauliflower.

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:17.040
I'm sorry.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:19.250
We'll do it at my step
mom's next time, OK?

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:20.618
It's better.

00:08:20.618 --> 00:08:22.910
If you still-- I mean, I
really wanted you to have fun,

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:24.200
I'm sorry.

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:26.378
It was fun, Eddie.

00:08:26.378 --> 00:08:27.420
I had a really nice trip.

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:33.190
The last hour was weird, but
overall still an A minus.

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:34.610
Yeah.

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:36.860
Your house is better.

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:38.929
You should come over.

00:08:38.929 --> 00:08:39.971
It's just two hours away.

00:08:39.971 --> 00:08:41.346
My parents would
love to see you.

00:08:41.346 --> 00:08:42.020
They miss you.

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:42.590
Yeah, sure.

00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:45.173
I bet my school bus would take
me to Brooklyn if I asked nice,

00:08:45.173 --> 00:08:46.620
right?

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:49.160
Is it such a crazy thought?

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:50.846
Coming to stay.

00:08:50.846 --> 00:08:52.760
It'd be sick.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:54.510
No one would mess with
me with you around.

00:08:57.310 --> 00:08:58.533
What?

00:08:58.533 --> 00:08:59.950
People are still
messing with you?

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:06.510
It's just what they do.

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:09.530
Who am I to stop
people from having fun?

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:12.810
Oh, you shouldn't let
people fuck with you, Peter.

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:15.690
You need to respect yourself.

00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:17.340
Peter makes a face.

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:19.190
They sit in this obvious irony.

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:24.530
You really think
I could help you?

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:27.438
Is that so hard to believe?

00:09:27.438 --> 00:09:28.585
I don't know.

00:09:28.585 --> 00:09:30.960
Sometimes I feel like I only
make things worse, you know?

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:32.380
I make people worse.

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:34.273
Horse shit, Edgar.

00:09:34.273 --> 00:09:36.315
Without you, I never
would've tried wearing hats.

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:40.150
You do look good in hats.

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:45.580
We help each other.

00:09:45.580 --> 00:09:49.980
If you move to New York,
we'd help each other.

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:51.090
Yeah.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:52.940
And how would you help me?

00:09:52.940 --> 00:09:55.990
I'd help people to get
to know you, get to like

00:09:55.990 --> 00:09:57.250
you, the real you.

00:09:57.250 --> 00:09:58.770
You saying I'm not likeable?

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:00.890
No.

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:02.096
Your face is.

00:10:02.096 --> 00:10:03.090
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:04.260
Oh.

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:05.680
Don't make me laugh.

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:07.840
OK, don't make me leave.

00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:12.710
Peter smiles.

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:14.400
Edgar smiles back.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.660
They relax for a moment and
look up at the trees slowly

00:10:17.660 --> 00:10:18.920
dropping leaves.

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:21.560
Peter sighs.

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:25.010
I mean, come on.

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:27.980
That's not beautiful
to you, Eddie?

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:30.740
They both get lost in the sight.

00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:31.910
Peter takes Edgar's hand.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:37.760
You think you'll come to
the beach again this summer?

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:40.100
A trail of melted
ice and blood drips

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:43.700
from the bag of cauliflower
and down Edgar's face.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:46.700
He notices it and
his body tenses.

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:48.470
His breathing catches.

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:51.230
He tries to stay calm.

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:53.200
Yeah, Pete?

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:56.500
Peter sees Edgar getting upset.

00:10:56.500 --> 00:10:57.500
Hey.

00:10:57.500 --> 00:10:58.965
Hey, do you you think, um--

00:10:58.965 --> 00:11:00.590
could they pick you
up down the street?

00:11:00.590 --> 00:11:01.910
Could you go down the street?

00:11:01.910 --> 00:11:02.660
I don't, I don't--

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:04.150
I mean, um.

00:11:04.150 --> 00:11:06.725
Edgar.

00:11:06.725 --> 00:11:08.990
A tear drops from
Edgar's good eye.

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:09.690
Please?

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:10.460
Pete, please?

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:13.340
I mean, they can't--

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:16.760
Edgar stands up and looks
away fighting back tears.

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:18.710
Peter stands and gets close.

00:11:18.710 --> 00:11:20.250
Edgar backs away.

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:21.060
No, come on.

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:21.560
Just stop.

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:23.580
I just-- I don't
want him to see me.

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:25.550
I'm no-- I don't want to like--

00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:28.890
Edgar drops the bag, faces
the wall and hangs his head.

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:31.730
Peter turns him around
and holds on to him.

00:11:31.730 --> 00:11:33.920
Edgar buries his face
into Peter's shoulder

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:35.240
and chokes back sobs.

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:39.168
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:11:39.168 --> 00:11:41.140
I mean, I didn't
do anything, man.

00:11:41.140 --> 00:11:42.440
I didn't even do anything.

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:45.380
What the fuck is
wrong with me, Pete?

00:11:45.380 --> 00:11:47.570
What the fuck is wrong with me?

00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:50.120
Peter rubs Edgar's head close.

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:52.130
Nothing.

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:55.180
Nothing is wrong
with you, Edgar.

00:11:55.180 --> 00:11:55.680
Nothing.

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:01.520
Edgar hugs him back.

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:05.510
Peter whispers in his ear.

00:12:05.510 --> 00:12:06.721
Leaves fall.

00:12:06.721 --> 00:12:10.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:13.430
Jane Therese is a
2020 Nicholl Fellow.

00:12:13.430 --> 00:12:16.730
Her winning screenplay
is "Sins of my Father."

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:18.800
Based on a true
story, the script

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:22.040
follows a young woman in Ireland
who grapples with the love

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:23.870
that she has for her
father after bringing

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:28.130
charges of abuse against him,
which shatters a country.

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:31.010
Jane is a photojournalist
who's covered stories ranging

00:12:31.010 --> 00:12:33.200
from a democracy
uprising in Haiti

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:36.380
to the events of
September 11, 2001.

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:38.900
As a writer, she scripted
"Sins of My Father"

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:41.240
to give voice to an
epidemic of abuse that

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:44.060
impacts families in Ireland,
as well as communities

00:12:44.060 --> 00:12:45.590
across the globe.

00:12:45.590 --> 00:12:49.340
Here's Jane in her own words.

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:52.940
I've been storytelling all of
my life in different formats.

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:55.130
I love screenwriting
and I love to be

00:12:55.130 --> 00:12:57.810
able to express myself that way.

00:12:57.810 --> 00:13:00.920
So I was in the
Athena Lab in 2019

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:03.620
and we were having a
roundtable discussion

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:06.740
on the behavioral
characteristics of a predator.

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:10.800
96% of victims recognize
their predator.

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:13.360
They've been coaxed,
they've been manipulated,

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:16.340
they've been persuaded
all under the distorted

00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:18.590
guise of love and trust.

00:13:18.590 --> 00:13:21.710
So what I wanted to do with
our protagonist, Imogen,

00:13:21.710 --> 00:13:25.102
was come from the experiences
of the child within her.

00:13:25.102 --> 00:13:26.810
The scene that you're
about ready to hear

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:29.450
takes place inside
the courthouse.

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:31.730
Imogen, her sister,
and her husband

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:33.800
are sequestered in a side room.

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:35.570
This is the day
that Imogen is going

00:13:35.570 --> 00:13:38.750
to be able to address the
judge for the first time.

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:41.630
In walks the investigator
and a social worker

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:44.030
to discuss what
actually is going to be

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:45.800
said inside the courtroom.

00:13:45.800 --> 00:13:48.980
This is the first time Imogen
realizes the consequences

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:50.780
of her actions
and that nobody is

00:13:50.780 --> 00:13:52.400
going to be leaving unscathed.

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:56.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:58.580
Interior courthouse
witness suite.

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:00.580
A sequestered room
with no windows.

00:14:00.580 --> 00:14:03.050
There is coffee,
tea, and danish.

00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:07.230
A couch, table and chairs,
magazines, bathroom,

00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:08.580
and a clock.

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:12.330
Everything one could want
while awaiting their fate.

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:13.940
So help yourself to anything.

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:16.920
It'll be a bit of a wait.

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:21.150
I do want to prepare you so that
there's no misunderstandings.

00:14:21.150 --> 00:14:22.560
It's just a bit nerve wracking.

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:24.960
Imogen is having a
minor panic attack.

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:26.170
Deep breaths.

00:14:26.170 --> 00:14:28.290
Barrett softens.

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:31.640
You're doing fine, Imogen.

00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:32.780
Yeah, right.

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:35.650
It's not you who has to show
your face afterwards, is it?

00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:38.510
No, no it's not.

00:14:38.510 --> 00:14:40.340
Imogen, your
father's defense will

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:42.560
try to plead a motion
for continuance

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:44.580
binding your father's
medical records.

00:14:44.580 --> 00:14:46.610
Imogen and Colleen scoff.

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:47.540
They won't get it.

00:14:47.540 --> 00:14:48.770
Excuse me?

00:14:48.770 --> 00:14:50.240
I can't go through this again.

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:52.010
This is happening
today if I have

00:14:52.010 --> 00:14:54.830
to follow the judge into
the jacks to plead my case.

00:14:54.830 --> 00:14:58.250
Let's just hope it
doesn't come to that.

00:14:58.250 --> 00:14:59.884
Relax, I'll be right back.

00:14:59.884 --> 00:15:00.730
[DOOR CLOSES]

00:15:00.730 --> 00:15:02.140
Barrett leaves.

00:15:02.140 --> 00:15:04.910
It's just Eamon, Colleen,
and Imogen alone.

00:15:04.910 --> 00:15:06.290
Eamon tries a danish.

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:08.510
He approves, offering
one to Imogen.

00:15:08.510 --> 00:15:10.165
No, my stomach is all twisted.

00:15:10.165 --> 00:15:13.670
Oh, the nerve of him.

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:15.350
Imogen lights a cigarette.

00:15:15.350 --> 00:15:17.630
Eamon fills his plate and sits.

00:15:17.630 --> 00:15:19.120
Anxiety grows.

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:21.290
Collen pulls a pot brownie
from her coat pocket

00:15:21.290 --> 00:15:22.360
and picks at it.

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:24.410
Eamon eyes her.

00:15:24.410 --> 00:15:27.077
You want some to
take the edge off?

00:15:27.077 --> 00:15:28.910
She breaks off a piece,
handing it to Eamon,

00:15:28.910 --> 00:15:31.690
only to have Imogen swoop
down and squash the morsel.

00:15:31.690 --> 00:15:32.900
Jesus.

00:15:32.900 --> 00:15:33.947
What the fuck?

00:15:33.947 --> 00:15:35.280
Giving me a bloody heart attack.

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:37.947
This is maddening enough without
you bringing your dope in here.

00:15:37.947 --> 00:15:41.060
OK, OK, OK.

00:15:41.060 --> 00:15:42.560
We hold on the
three for what seems

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:44.660
like an endless amount of time.

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:47.240
Imogen shoots
Eamon the evil eye.

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:49.870
He begs forgiveness by
making another offer of food.

00:15:49.870 --> 00:15:53.270
Imogen and picks up the
magazine, looking through it.

00:15:53.270 --> 00:15:55.370
She sets it down in paces.

00:15:55.370 --> 00:15:57.905
Colleen takes a small drawing
pad and pencil from her purse

00:15:57.905 --> 00:15:59.840
and sketches.

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:01.710
We feel the strain.

00:16:01.710 --> 00:16:03.560
Imogen picks at her scab.

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:05.510
The clock's second
hand jerks forward,

00:16:05.510 --> 00:16:07.490
catching Imogen's attention.

00:16:07.490 --> 00:16:10.550
She watches it tick
around its circumference.

00:16:10.550 --> 00:16:13.090
Enter Barrett and Erin Lawlor.

00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:15.230
Imogen brightens.

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:16.572
How are you, Imogen?

00:16:16.572 --> 00:16:18.530
I'll be representing the
sexual assault victims

00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:19.930
unit on your case.

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:21.680
How much longer?

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:25.620
He'll plead, then we
go right to sentencing.

00:16:25.620 --> 00:16:28.125
Imogen's now uncomfortable
with the idea.

00:16:28.125 --> 00:16:29.750
Colleen wraps her
arm around an Imogen,

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:32.300
pulling her body towards
her for reassurance.

00:16:32.300 --> 00:16:37.130
Hey, this is what
you wanted, right?

00:16:37.130 --> 00:16:40.040
Interior courthouse
witness suite, same.

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:42.830
Barrett sits at the table
and opens his briefcase.

00:16:42.830 --> 00:16:46.730
He lays out a redacted
copy of her statement.

00:16:46.730 --> 00:16:48.590
So I'm going to jump right in.

00:16:48.590 --> 00:16:53.780
Your victim impact statement,
you mentioned Eugene's death.

00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:56.510
My dad's the reason
Gene killed himself.

00:16:56.510 --> 00:16:58.166
He was tormented.

00:16:58.166 --> 00:16:59.390
OK.

00:16:59.390 --> 00:17:03.060
Your father wants that removed.

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:03.680
Why?

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:05.472
Eugene would be here
if it weren't for him.

00:17:05.472 --> 00:17:06.470
He confessed to us.

00:17:06.470 --> 00:17:08.482
Jean couldn't stand
him after what he did.

00:17:08.482 --> 00:17:10.940
Well, there's no absolute proof
he wouldn't have turned out

00:17:10.940 --> 00:17:12.283
like that on his own.

00:17:12.283 --> 00:17:14.060
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:15.030
Yeah, I bet.

00:17:15.030 --> 00:17:16.470
Are you joking me, man?

00:17:16.470 --> 00:17:18.560
Ladies, I have no
control over this.

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:25.800
Let's keep the focus on
Imogen. The cemetery.

00:17:25.800 --> 00:17:28.900
Balking at the absurdity,
Colleen flinches.

00:17:28.900 --> 00:17:30.610
Fucking me on his mom
and pop's grave too

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:32.063
sacrilegious for him now?

00:17:32.063 --> 00:17:33.730
What good is the
victim's statement when

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:35.470
I can't use the bloody thing?

00:17:35.470 --> 00:17:37.910
Erin soothes Imogen.

00:17:37.910 --> 00:17:39.980
And the time at Killiney Beach?

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:41.910
Killiney too.

00:17:41.910 --> 00:17:43.950
Imogen throws your hands up.

00:17:43.950 --> 00:17:44.950
For God's sake, man.

00:17:44.950 --> 00:17:46.930
If I don't sit, I will
surely walk out of here

00:17:46.930 --> 00:17:48.370
and kill him with my own hands.

00:17:48.370 --> 00:17:50.050
Then there won't be
a need for a trial.

00:17:50.050 --> 00:17:51.160
Tell him that.

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:55.470
We have enough evidence, Imogen.
It's just the statement is all.

00:17:55.470 --> 00:17:57.120
There is one more thing.

00:17:57.120 --> 00:17:59.390
For the love of God.

00:17:59.390 --> 00:17:59.890
Ane.

00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:09.620
What about Ane?

00:18:09.620 --> 00:18:12.450
The courts have your records.

00:18:12.450 --> 00:18:16.440
He petitioned for
them, remember?

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:17.810
She's right.

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:19.500
This is her doing.

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:22.520
Barrett notices the
time on the clock.

00:18:22.520 --> 00:18:23.570
We need to go.

00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:25.550
Please, Detective, you
can't say anything.

00:18:25.550 --> 00:18:26.550
I spoke in confidence.

00:18:26.550 --> 00:18:28.010
Tell him anything else.

00:18:28.010 --> 00:18:30.800
I'm sorry, Imogen,
it's out of our hands.

00:18:30.800 --> 00:18:33.470
You want him to
go to jail, right?

00:18:33.470 --> 00:18:35.490
Barrett holds the door
open for the women.

00:18:35.490 --> 00:18:37.910
Colleen lovingly puts
her arm around Imogen,

00:18:37.910 --> 00:18:40.910
leading her into the
courthouse hallway

00:18:40.910 --> 00:18:43.730
where Eamon is waiting with
a slew of news journalists.

00:18:43.730 --> 00:18:46.160
The barrage of news
crew flood Imogen. Eamon

00:18:46.160 --> 00:18:47.780
rushes to her side.

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:49.820
Barrett escorts them
inside the courtroom.

00:18:49.820 --> 00:18:51.162
Reporters follow.

00:18:51.162 --> 00:18:53.522
[PHOTOS SNAPPING]

00:18:53.522 --> 00:18:55.410
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:18:55.410 --> 00:18:57.160
Story is everything.

00:18:57.160 --> 00:19:00.960
It can make one person laugh or
it can light up a crowded room.

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:03.390
It can give voice to a
community or speak the truth

00:19:03.390 --> 00:19:06.540
of a town, a city, a nation.

00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:08.430
It can bring the
difficult past to life

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:11.160
and imagine the best
possible future.

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:13.650
Story is everything.

00:19:13.650 --> 00:19:17.970
But nothing unless they include
all voices, especially yours.

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:20.430
For 35 years, the Academy
Nicholl Fellowships

00:19:20.430 --> 00:19:22.620
in Screenwriting has
lifted up storytellers

00:19:22.620 --> 00:19:25.200
and championed
inclusive storytelling.

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:27.510
Over 170 Nicholl
Fellowships have

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:31.200
been awarded since the
program began in 1986.

00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:32.910
Nicholl Fellows have
gone on to become

00:19:32.910 --> 00:19:36.660
some of the most impactful
storytellers in film.

00:19:36.660 --> 00:19:38.700
Becoming a Nicholl
Fellow means the start

00:19:38.700 --> 00:19:41.520
of an incredible journey
to tell you a story,

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:43.830
to write a feature
length screenplay,

00:19:43.830 --> 00:19:47.850
to mentor with Academy members
and receive a $35,000 cash

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:49.350
prize.

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:51.810
And today, we're
surrounded by stories

00:19:51.810 --> 00:19:55.830
of unprecedented trial, triumph,
and everything in between.

00:19:55.830 --> 00:19:58.740
What makes your story
new, different, and unique

00:19:58.740 --> 00:20:03.420
is more fierce, necessary,
and urgent than ever.

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:07.200
Your story can entertain,
educate, empower.

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:10.350
You can do all that and more.

00:20:10.350 --> 00:20:13.780
Your story can change the world.

00:20:13.780 --> 00:20:15.720
And the world needs to hear you.

00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:17.670
Now more than ever.

00:20:17.670 --> 00:20:20.210
Start your journey
today in oscars.org.

00:20:20.210 --> 00:20:23.890
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:20:23.890 --> 00:20:27.150
Vanar Jaddou is a
2020 Nicholl fellow.

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:30.290
His winning screenplay
is "Goodbye, Iraq."

00:20:30.290 --> 00:20:32.270
Inspired by true
events, the script

00:20:32.270 --> 00:20:34.790
chronicles the struggles
of a paranoid ex-solider,

00:20:34.790 --> 00:20:38.090
and his fiery daughter who
flee from Iraq to the US

00:20:38.090 --> 00:20:41.270
while they're hunted by Saddam
Hussein's ruthless regime.

00:20:41.270 --> 00:20:44.030
The son of brave
refugees from Iraq, Vanar

00:20:44.030 --> 00:20:46.130
was galvanized to
write "Goodbye, Iraq"

00:20:46.130 --> 00:20:48.020
by the stories of
his grandfather,

00:20:48.020 --> 00:20:50.630
as well as the mostly unheard
voices of many Kurdish

00:20:50.630 --> 00:20:52.340
and Chaldean immigrants.

00:20:52.340 --> 00:20:55.500
Here's Vanar in his own words.

00:20:55.500 --> 00:20:58.400
I've been screenwriting
for about nine years.

00:20:58.400 --> 00:21:01.340
When I was young, my mom
would make me and my brothers

00:21:01.340 --> 00:21:03.470
either draw or
write a short story

00:21:03.470 --> 00:21:05.660
before we went outside
to play sports.

00:21:05.660 --> 00:21:08.750
And I think that's where
imagination and originality

00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:09.950
were born for me.

00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:12.350
And my dad, he had
a small video store

00:21:12.350 --> 00:21:14.210
where I used to work
on the weekends.

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:16.940
And by the time I was 15,
I had seen more movies

00:21:16.940 --> 00:21:19.610
than was probably healthy
for a teenage kid.

00:21:19.610 --> 00:21:22.790
There were so many untold
and undocumented stories

00:21:22.790 --> 00:21:27.080
that my father, and grandfather,
my mom had about Iraq

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:28.130
in their village.

00:21:28.130 --> 00:21:31.760
There was so much joy there,
but there's also a lot of pain

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:33.080
and a lot of horror.

00:21:33.080 --> 00:21:36.170
I wanted to write something
that was set in that world.

00:21:36.170 --> 00:21:38.230
In this scene from
"Goodbye, Iraq,"

00:21:38.230 --> 00:21:40.310
Manzin and Noor, a
father and daughter

00:21:40.310 --> 00:21:43.040
on the run from Saddam's
forces, have narrowly

00:21:43.040 --> 00:21:46.940
escaped death from a missile and
cocktail of chemical weapons.

00:21:46.940 --> 00:21:49.070
Now in a mosque with
other survivors,

00:21:49.070 --> 00:21:51.200
but a place with none
of their own people,

00:21:51.200 --> 00:21:53.870
they must attempt to regather
themselves and determine

00:21:53.870 --> 00:21:56.240
who should and
shouldn't be trusted

00:21:56.240 --> 00:21:59.180
as they begin their
dangerous refuge to America.

00:21:59.180 --> 00:22:03.060
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:22:03.060 --> 00:22:03.560
Interior.

00:22:03.560 --> 00:22:05.580
Mosque, Badinan countryside day.

00:22:05.580 --> 00:22:08.440
Kurds clean wounds, use
oxygen tanks to breathe,

00:22:08.440 --> 00:22:10.080
perform small surgeries.

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:11.970
Many blistered and
swollen from the cocktail

00:22:11.970 --> 00:22:13.380
of gas and chemicals.

00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:14.580
Even more are blind.

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:16.820
They pray.

00:22:16.820 --> 00:22:20.080
Mahzen slumps, pellet
lodged in his left shoulder.

00:22:20.080 --> 00:22:21.440
Cuts caked in blood.

00:22:21.440 --> 00:22:22.420
Road burns.

00:22:22.420 --> 00:22:24.970
Past scars etched
like railroad tracks.

00:22:24.970 --> 00:22:27.760
Noor stares wide-eyed
at his wounds.

00:22:27.760 --> 00:22:29.920
Should I get someone
else to do it?

00:22:29.920 --> 00:22:32.080
Noor wipes away a
tear, grabs a bottle

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:33.910
of Arak (liquor)
laying around and pours

00:22:33.910 --> 00:22:35.710
some on Mahzen's shoulder.

00:22:35.710 --> 00:22:37.720
Fresh blood splashes
out of the hole.

00:22:37.720 --> 00:22:42.100
As Noor preps a needle and
thread, her focus sharpens.

00:22:42.100 --> 00:22:43.300
Hold still.

00:22:43.300 --> 00:22:47.430
She sews into the
skin above the pellet.

00:22:47.430 --> 00:22:49.682
Mom teach you this?

00:22:49.682 --> 00:22:51.417
Yeah.

00:22:51.417 --> 00:22:52.000
Tugs a thread.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:52.825
He grimaces.

00:22:52.825 --> 00:22:53.700
She pulls it through.

00:22:56.750 --> 00:22:59.390
You remind me so much of her.

00:22:59.390 --> 00:23:02.440
Most of our people,
they're very conservative.

00:23:02.440 --> 00:23:06.370
But your mom was adventurous.

00:23:06.370 --> 00:23:07.100
Noor smiles.

00:23:07.100 --> 00:23:08.400
She refocuses on his wound.

00:23:11.340 --> 00:23:16.480
Thank you for earlier,
for telling me the truth.

00:23:16.480 --> 00:23:18.470
You deserve to know.

00:23:18.470 --> 00:23:21.970
Ahead, Peshmerga huddle,
dressed in desert camouflage.

00:23:21.970 --> 00:23:25.360
We have to leave,
we can't fight gas.

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:28.040
And bullets, we can
dodge no longer.

00:23:28.040 --> 00:23:29.990
They will come
for the survivors.

00:23:29.990 --> 00:23:32.210
We must get these
people to Turkey.

00:23:32.210 --> 00:23:34.900
Noor listens in as Mazin
digs into his backpack

00:23:34.900 --> 00:23:36.170
for something.

00:23:36.170 --> 00:23:39.200
By nightfall, these
villages will all be bombed.

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:41.750
They have families
too, where we're

00:23:41.750 --> 00:23:45.060
going, hiding in the mountains.

00:23:45.060 --> 00:23:48.350
Noor fidgets with the needle
unnerved, both at the violence

00:23:48.350 --> 00:23:50.250
and how privy he is to it.

00:23:50.250 --> 00:23:54.440
Manzin uncaps a pill bottle
and pops an antibiotic.

00:23:54.440 --> 00:23:57.270
It doesn't have to be like that.

00:23:57.270 --> 00:23:59.270
There doesn't have to
be violence everywhere

00:23:59.270 --> 00:24:00.330
all the time.

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:03.010
Noor, these people are
fighting for their freedom,

00:24:03.010 --> 00:24:04.940
for their own.

00:24:04.940 --> 00:24:07.570
Well, that's not living.

00:24:07.570 --> 00:24:10.600
As Mahzen considers that, a
red wizened face in a hijab

00:24:10.600 --> 00:24:12.250
scuffles into the dim light.

00:24:12.250 --> 00:24:12.910
Roza.

00:24:12.910 --> 00:24:14.780
She speaks Arabic.

00:24:14.780 --> 00:24:20.060
I wanted to see how
you two were doing.

00:24:20.060 --> 00:24:21.540
How bad is it?

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:22.400
Mahzen observes her.

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:25.090
The deep cracks on her
forehand and around her eyes

00:24:25.090 --> 00:24:26.980
are smeared with dust.

00:24:26.980 --> 00:24:28.620
He's had worse.

00:24:28.620 --> 00:24:31.510
Roza hands Mahzen
a canteen of water.

00:24:31.510 --> 00:24:33.310
He assesses her.

00:24:33.310 --> 00:24:35.040
Her hijab.

00:24:35.040 --> 00:24:37.480
Doesn't accept.

00:24:37.480 --> 00:24:40.880
It's dangerous to be
a Kurd in this world.

00:24:40.880 --> 00:24:43.710
To come to these villages,
it's irresponsible.

00:24:43.710 --> 00:24:45.200
Mahzen surveys the mosque.

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:46.770
Kurds vomit blood.

00:24:46.770 --> 00:24:48.870
Some of their skin
starting to blister.

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:50.550
Eyes beginning to bleed.

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:52.680
Why don't you worry
about your own people?

00:24:52.680 --> 00:24:56.130
She understands his English, but
continues to respond in Arabic.

00:24:56.130 --> 00:24:57.880
Don't you have any sense?

00:24:57.880 --> 00:24:59.940
Look, we're just
passing through.

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:00.660
Passing through.

00:25:03.350 --> 00:25:06.612
I see.

00:25:06.612 --> 00:25:09.920
So you're him.

00:25:09.920 --> 00:25:12.290
Mahzen stares at her like a
predator caught off guard.

00:25:12.290 --> 00:25:15.350
He scans the mosque as if
waiting for a slew of hyenas

00:25:15.350 --> 00:25:16.460
to encircle him.

00:25:16.460 --> 00:25:17.690
That's good enough, Noor.

00:25:17.690 --> 00:25:18.960
But I'm not done yet.

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:20.360
It's fine.

00:25:20.360 --> 00:25:22.320
Knot it.

00:25:22.320 --> 00:25:25.200
Noor pulls the strings
end, brings as much flesh

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:28.220
as she can together
before knotting it.

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:30.420
Mahzen wipes his body
with his ripped shirt,

00:25:30.420 --> 00:25:33.480
slips it on, throws
a bomber over it.

00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:34.710
Where are you going?

00:25:34.710 --> 00:25:35.540
America.

00:25:35.540 --> 00:25:36.980
Noor.

00:25:36.980 --> 00:25:38.570
Mahzen glowers at her.

00:25:38.570 --> 00:25:41.630
He forces the oversized
bulletproof vest over her shirt

00:25:41.630 --> 00:25:44.070
then grabs her arm.

00:25:44.070 --> 00:25:44.990
Let's go.

00:25:44.990 --> 00:25:48.580
You should be careful who
sees you out of this country.

00:25:48.580 --> 00:25:51.655
They may have other ideas when
they find out who you are.

00:25:51.655 --> 00:25:53.530
The Peshmerga will take
us over the mountain.

00:25:53.530 --> 00:25:54.550
Then what?

00:25:54.550 --> 00:25:57.940
You have to get to Greece
to get off his radar.

00:25:57.940 --> 00:25:59.890
What is it that you want?

00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:01.910
Just to help.

00:26:01.910 --> 00:26:04.340
As you tried to do for us all.

00:26:04.340 --> 00:26:07.060
As your daughter did for me.

00:26:07.060 --> 00:26:11.790
Roza motions for them to
follow then walks ahead.

00:26:11.790 --> 00:26:14.040
I trust her.

00:26:14.040 --> 00:26:14.880
We don't know her.

00:26:17.640 --> 00:26:21.810
Noor follows Roza,
leaving Mahzen

00:26:21.810 --> 00:26:22.930
to deliberate on his own.

00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:29.360
Beth Curry is a
2020 Nicholl Fellow.

00:26:29.360 --> 00:26:31.610
Her winning
screenplay is "Lemon."

00:26:31.610 --> 00:26:34.970
The script explores the trials
of a 5-year-old girl who,

00:26:34.970 --> 00:26:37.100
after the death of her
agoraphobic mother,

00:26:37.100 --> 00:26:40.880
ventures outside for the first
time in search of her father.

00:26:40.880 --> 00:26:43.070
Beth is a former
Broadway performer.

00:26:43.070 --> 00:26:45.050
While on tour with
"Young Frankenstein"

00:26:45.050 --> 00:26:48.260
and sitting backstage wearing
a Bride of Frankenstein wig,

00:26:48.260 --> 00:26:50.390
Beth wrote one of
her first scripts.

00:26:50.390 --> 00:26:52.850
She believes more
than ever that stories

00:26:52.850 --> 00:26:55.520
need to make a difference by
lifting up voices that have

00:26:55.520 --> 00:26:58.140
gone unheard for far too long.

00:26:58.140 --> 00:26:59.570
Here's Beth in her own words.

00:26:59.570 --> 00:27:01.860
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:03.540
I first got into writing.

00:27:03.540 --> 00:27:06.060
I was on the National tour
of "Young Frankenstein"

00:27:06.060 --> 00:27:07.410
playing the Madeleine Khan.

00:27:07.410 --> 00:27:09.750
And I would do a number
in the first act.

00:27:09.750 --> 00:27:13.050
And then I would go
offstage for an hour.

00:27:13.050 --> 00:27:15.960
And then one day
I started writing.

00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:20.370
The feeling I had at the
end of it was so fulfilling.

00:27:20.370 --> 00:27:23.610
And so from that moment
on, I was addicted.

00:27:23.610 --> 00:27:27.900
My mission as a writer is to
tell stories and give voice

00:27:27.900 --> 00:27:30.630
to characters that
don't have a voice

00:27:30.630 --> 00:27:33.090
or to tell stories that
haven't been told yet.

00:27:33.090 --> 00:27:35.460
And just have a little
more kindness for somebody

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:37.530
that you don't understand.

00:27:37.530 --> 00:27:40.500
So this is a scene
from "Lemon" where

00:27:40.500 --> 00:27:44.490
Lemon is having pancakes
with her social worker

00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:45.510
and with Michael.

00:27:45.510 --> 00:27:47.730
And she's affectionately
named him Fluffy Head.

00:27:47.730 --> 00:27:49.710
She wants to be
adopted by Fluffy Head,

00:27:49.710 --> 00:27:52.350
and it's very clear to her
that they belong together,

00:27:52.350 --> 00:27:55.710
whereas he has suffered
a lot of loss in his life

00:27:55.710 --> 00:27:59.640
and wants a little distance
even though he's really

00:27:59.640 --> 00:28:00.660
drawn to Lemon.

00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:03.370
And they have an
undeniable connection.

00:28:03.370 --> 00:28:07.080
So this is a scene
where their desires

00:28:07.080 --> 00:28:09.520
are in conflict over pancakes.

00:28:09.520 --> 00:28:13.640
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:28:13.640 --> 00:28:15.860
Interior, Sheila's
office, hours later.

00:28:15.860 --> 00:28:16.650
[RINGING]

00:28:16.650 --> 00:28:17.900
Sheila's phone rings.

00:28:17.900 --> 00:28:20.090
Asleep on her desk, she
wakes up at the start.

00:28:20.090 --> 00:28:22.204
She groggily answers.

00:28:22.204 --> 00:28:23.120
Hello?

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:24.990
She looks over to
where Lemon was.

00:28:24.990 --> 00:28:26.060
She's gone.

00:28:26.060 --> 00:28:28.640
She runs out of the
office looking for Lemon.

00:28:28.640 --> 00:28:29.870
Hold on.

00:28:29.870 --> 00:28:31.370
She runs out of the office.

00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:33.080
She asks an officer.

00:28:33.080 --> 00:28:34.430
Where's Lemon?

00:28:34.430 --> 00:28:36.510
He points around the corner.

00:28:36.510 --> 00:28:38.430
She sees Lemon with PJ, four.

00:28:38.430 --> 00:28:41.540
The girl laughing, pretending
to talk into Pip's old phone.

00:28:41.540 --> 00:28:44.180
Sheila lets out a
big sigh of relief.

00:28:44.180 --> 00:28:46.370
I'm sorry, who is this again?

00:28:46.370 --> 00:28:48.560
Exterior park, continuous.

00:28:48.560 --> 00:28:51.220
Michael sits on a bench
as he talks to Sheila.

00:28:51.220 --> 00:28:52.810
Intercut call.

00:28:52.810 --> 00:28:55.640
Hi, it's Michael Archer.

00:28:55.640 --> 00:28:57.650
Lemon was in my closet.

00:28:57.650 --> 00:28:58.550
Oh, hi.

00:28:58.550 --> 00:29:00.560
How are you doing?

00:29:00.560 --> 00:29:03.682
I'm still a little overwhelmed.

00:29:03.682 --> 00:29:06.620
Uh-huh, I can imagine.

00:29:06.620 --> 00:29:09.770
Paul at the police
station gave me a card.

00:29:09.770 --> 00:29:15.046
And I know this is weird, but I
just wanted to check on Lemon.

00:29:15.046 --> 00:29:17.780
Well, she's been asking for you.

00:29:17.780 --> 00:29:19.280
She has?

00:29:19.280 --> 00:29:20.360
Oh yes.

00:29:20.360 --> 00:29:22.750
It's Fluffy Head this
and Fluffy Head that.

00:29:22.750 --> 00:29:24.020
Fluffy Head, yeah.

00:29:24.020 --> 00:29:25.730
That's funny.

00:29:25.730 --> 00:29:29.310
I, uh, I was just wondering
if I could meet you

00:29:29.310 --> 00:29:33.750
both for breakfast sometime?

00:29:33.750 --> 00:29:35.790
How about today?

00:29:35.790 --> 00:29:39.500
Interior Pancake Pals
Restaurant, moments later.

00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:41.840
Lemon is poised over her
chocolate chip pancakes

00:29:41.840 --> 00:29:43.310
in her protective gear.

00:29:43.310 --> 00:29:46.010
Her scarf is pushed
down around her neck.

00:29:46.010 --> 00:29:48.470
And so I ran away
because she's mean

00:29:48.470 --> 00:29:50.090
and she might steal my soul.

00:29:50.090 --> 00:29:52.880
Ellen is not going
to steal your soul.

00:29:52.880 --> 00:29:55.670
Lemon's mom was
agoraphobic and she

00:29:55.670 --> 00:29:57.770
conditioned Lemon to
fear going outside saying

00:29:57.770 --> 00:29:59.260
people would steal her soul.

00:29:59.260 --> 00:30:00.140
You knew?

00:30:00.140 --> 00:30:02.240
I am aware of that.

00:30:02.240 --> 00:30:04.310
I am just filling Michael in.

00:30:04.310 --> 00:30:05.920
Fluffy Head!

00:30:05.920 --> 00:30:07.730
Fluffy Head.

00:30:07.730 --> 00:30:09.786
I'm not going to get
away from that, am I?

00:30:09.786 --> 00:30:11.070
No.

00:30:11.070 --> 00:30:13.890
Anyway, Ellen yells and
she throws things around.

00:30:13.890 --> 00:30:15.090
Your foster mother?

00:30:15.090 --> 00:30:18.170
Those are very strong
accusations, Lemon.

00:30:18.170 --> 00:30:20.012
Well, I hid in the
closet and asked Hyla

00:30:20.012 --> 00:30:21.720
and she told me that
I'll get used to it.

00:30:21.720 --> 00:30:24.060
But I don't want
to get used to it.

00:30:24.060 --> 00:30:25.710
A waiter comes up to the table.

00:30:25.710 --> 00:30:27.360
Can I get you anything else?

00:30:27.360 --> 00:30:29.940
Upon seeing him, Lemon puts
up her scarf around her mouth

00:30:29.940 --> 00:30:32.560
and puts her head on the table.

00:30:32.560 --> 00:30:35.110
How are those chocolate
chip pancakes?

00:30:35.110 --> 00:30:36.490
Lemon remains hidden.

00:30:36.490 --> 00:30:38.460
They were great.

00:30:38.460 --> 00:30:39.370
Mmm.

00:30:39.370 --> 00:30:41.170
We'll just take the bill.

00:30:41.170 --> 00:30:44.470
The waiter tears off the bill
and leaves it on the table.

00:30:44.470 --> 00:30:46.240
Is he gone?

00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:47.870
He's gone.

00:30:47.870 --> 00:30:49.360
Lemon looks up again.

00:30:49.360 --> 00:30:51.730
Lemon, no one is
going to steal--

00:30:51.730 --> 00:30:53.420
You don't know that.

00:30:53.420 --> 00:30:54.830
OK, it hasn't happened yet.

00:30:54.830 --> 00:30:56.770
So maybe it won't happen.

00:30:56.770 --> 00:30:58.830
Or maybe it will.

00:30:58.830 --> 00:31:02.440
Sheila and Michael
exchange a look.

00:31:02.440 --> 00:31:04.830
Well, what if-- what
if I live with you?

00:31:04.830 --> 00:31:07.090
Like for reals.

00:31:07.090 --> 00:31:08.982
Oh God, no.

00:31:08.982 --> 00:31:15.130
I-- I mean, I just wanted
to meet up and say hi.

00:31:15.130 --> 00:31:18.017
I didn't mean for you to
misconstrue my intentions.

00:31:18.017 --> 00:31:19.600
I just wanted to
check up on you and--

00:31:19.600 --> 00:31:22.450
You're saying a lot of big words
but all you need to say is yes.

00:31:22.450 --> 00:31:24.160
Lemon.

00:31:24.160 --> 00:31:28.600
I'm just-- I'm not in the
space right now to, um--

00:31:28.600 --> 00:31:30.490
Please, I can't go
back to Ellen's.

00:31:30.490 --> 00:31:33.580
Lemon, we need to let
Michael have some time.

00:31:33.580 --> 00:31:34.870
He's been through a lot.

00:31:34.870 --> 00:31:38.780
But I've been through a lot too.

00:31:38.780 --> 00:31:40.220
We both need hugs.

00:31:40.220 --> 00:31:42.200
Why can't I live with you?

00:31:42.200 --> 00:31:46.280
You're good, you saved that
bird, you won't steal my soul.

00:31:46.280 --> 00:31:48.050
Please.

00:31:48.050 --> 00:31:48.550
Please!

00:31:52.220 --> 00:31:53.317
I'm sorry, Lemon.

00:31:53.317 --> 00:31:54.150
I wish I could help.

00:31:59.990 --> 00:32:02.960
I'm going to go pay the bill.

00:32:02.960 --> 00:32:05.150
Lemon lays her head
down on the table.

00:32:05.150 --> 00:32:07.520
Michael looks at
Sheila apologetically

00:32:07.520 --> 00:32:08.996
as he walks to the counter.

00:32:08.996 --> 00:32:11.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:32:11.180 --> 00:32:13.780
Kate Marks is a
2020 Nicholl Fellow.

00:32:13.780 --> 00:32:16.420
Her winning screenplay
is "The Cow of Queens."

00:32:16.420 --> 00:32:19.630
The coming of age drama explores
how a young woman and her dying

00:32:19.630 --> 00:32:22.120
father struggle to save
an escaped slaughterhouse

00:32:22.120 --> 00:32:24.790
cow running for its life
on the streets of Queens.

00:32:24.790 --> 00:32:27.700
Kate loves to tell stories
that flip from hilarious

00:32:27.700 --> 00:32:29.110
to heartbreaking.

00:32:29.110 --> 00:32:31.330
Her deep desire to
use humor and strength

00:32:31.330 --> 00:32:33.360
to confront and
confound grief and loss

00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:36.070
is what helped to inspire
"The Cow of Queens."

00:32:36.070 --> 00:32:39.100
Here's Kate in her own words.

00:32:39.100 --> 00:32:43.360
I started out writing plays,
but the very first short

00:32:43.360 --> 00:32:46.000
that I made, I felt
like I was able to get

00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:49.090
inside the character in a
way that I had never been

00:32:49.090 --> 00:32:51.070
able to do as a playwright.

00:32:51.070 --> 00:32:54.310
"The Cow of Queens" is
inspired by two very different,

00:32:54.310 --> 00:32:57.850
but both true stories about
the fight to stay alive.

00:32:57.850 --> 00:33:00.050
The first one comes from my dad.

00:33:00.050 --> 00:33:02.000
My dad was my best friend.

00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:04.930
So when he got sick, I
couldn't leave his side.

00:33:04.930 --> 00:33:08.560
So after he died, I was
haunted by these questions.

00:33:08.560 --> 00:33:10.030
When do you fight?

00:33:10.030 --> 00:33:11.740
When do you accept?

00:33:11.740 --> 00:33:13.600
And how do you say goodbye?

00:33:13.600 --> 00:33:16.360
And then I heard
about a cow that

00:33:16.360 --> 00:33:19.240
escaped a local
slaughterhouse and had

00:33:19.240 --> 00:33:21.130
a field day running
from the police

00:33:21.130 --> 00:33:22.600
on the streets of Queens.

00:33:22.600 --> 00:33:26.740
I started to imagine my dad
and I as Don Quixote and Sancho

00:33:26.740 --> 00:33:30.370
Panza chasing an impossible
dream on a mission

00:33:30.370 --> 00:33:33.790
to right one last wrong
and save this cow.

00:33:33.790 --> 00:33:36.760
In this scene, you're going to
meet father, daughter duo Del

00:33:36.760 --> 00:33:40.210
and Sonya, in the midst
of a very rough morning

00:33:40.210 --> 00:33:44.410
after a very rough night
dealing with Del's failing body.

00:33:44.410 --> 00:33:48.430
They are just about to leave
for a doctor's appointment

00:33:48.430 --> 00:33:52.240
where they are going to get
the results of Del's PET scan

00:33:52.240 --> 00:33:55.705
and learn whether the last
round of chemo worked.

00:33:55.705 --> 00:33:58.680
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:33:58.680 --> 00:34:01.770
Interior kitchen, morning.

00:34:01.770 --> 00:34:04.480
You ready?

00:34:04.480 --> 00:34:06.560
I just got to iron my pants.

00:34:06.560 --> 00:34:07.960
We don't have time.

00:34:07.960 --> 00:34:11.159
I don't want to get bad
news in wrinkled pants.

00:34:11.159 --> 00:34:14.670
Can we please just wait to
hear what Dr. Schlossberg says?

00:34:14.670 --> 00:34:17.260
That guy's about as
useful as tits on a bull.

00:34:17.260 --> 00:34:19.000
Come on.

00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:22.659
I don't need some damn scan to
tell me parts are falling off.

00:34:22.659 --> 00:34:25.904
Sonya pushes the
eggs toward Del.

00:34:25.904 --> 00:34:28.280
It's cause you're not eating.

00:34:28.280 --> 00:34:31.210
No, it's cause I'm getting
the shit kicked out of me.

00:34:31.210 --> 00:34:33.060
Del shakes his head.

00:34:33.060 --> 00:34:34.730
Have one bite, at least.

00:34:38.120 --> 00:34:39.889
We're out of ketchup.

00:34:39.889 --> 00:34:42.308
Sonya whips out a bottle
and squirts a giant glob

00:34:42.308 --> 00:34:42.850
on the plate.

00:34:46.590 --> 00:34:47.909
I can't.

00:34:47.909 --> 00:34:49.260
Sonya scoops up the eggs.

00:34:49.260 --> 00:34:52.440
They jiggle on the spoon as
she brings them to Del's mouth.

00:34:52.440 --> 00:34:54.929
Jesus Christ, I'm not a baby.

00:34:54.929 --> 00:34:57.020
Del locks his lips shut.

00:34:57.020 --> 00:35:01.270
You have to get
something in your system.

00:35:01.270 --> 00:35:05.130
He slowly takes a bite.

00:35:05.130 --> 00:35:05.630
Thank you.

00:35:05.630 --> 00:35:08.580
He holds the food in his cheeks.

00:35:08.580 --> 00:35:09.930
Come on.

00:35:09.930 --> 00:35:11.580
Silence.

00:35:11.580 --> 00:35:13.420
Swallow it.

00:35:13.420 --> 00:35:17.244
He tries, but it
comes right back up.

00:35:17.244 --> 00:35:18.110
[SPITTING]

00:35:18.110 --> 00:35:22.180
Sonya slams a
spoon on the table.

00:35:22.180 --> 00:35:24.170
I told you, I can't do it.

00:35:24.170 --> 00:35:27.110
Sonya feels like she's
about to throw up.

00:35:27.110 --> 00:35:29.240
I'm sorry, sweet.

00:35:29.240 --> 00:35:32.720
Sonya rushes out.

00:35:32.720 --> 00:35:34.860
We're going to be late.

00:35:34.860 --> 00:35:36.860
Interior hallway, 10:30 AM.

00:35:36.860 --> 00:35:40.140
Del and Sonya stare down
a long set of stairs.

00:35:40.140 --> 00:35:43.200
Del leans on Sonya who's also
got a folded up wheelchair.

00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:45.870
Del whistles, they take
it one step at a time

00:35:45.870 --> 00:35:47.760
as they begin
their slow descent.

00:35:47.760 --> 00:35:50.670
A busy woman, 30s, races past
them with a mug of coffee,

00:35:50.670 --> 00:35:52.670
clicking in bright
red scuffed heels.

00:35:52.670 --> 00:35:54.150
Blisters forming.

00:35:54.150 --> 00:35:56.230
A couple of drops of
coffee splash out.

00:35:56.230 --> 00:35:58.830
They round the second landing
passing a fighting couple.

00:35:58.830 --> 00:36:00.000
A woman screams.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:02.310
Del stands at attention.

00:36:02.310 --> 00:36:03.940
It's nothing.

00:36:03.940 --> 00:36:05.020
How do you know?

00:36:05.020 --> 00:36:06.930
They do this all the time.

00:36:06.930 --> 00:36:08.055
Del looks back.

00:36:08.055 --> 00:36:09.930
The argument sounds like
the end of the world

00:36:09.930 --> 00:36:12.100
and it hits him hard.

00:36:12.100 --> 00:36:13.900
Exterior, street, moments later.

00:36:13.900 --> 00:36:16.060
Sonya pushes Del
in a wheelchair.

00:36:16.060 --> 00:36:18.340
Humiliated, he pulls
his hat over his eyes

00:36:18.340 --> 00:36:20.500
as they pass a friendly face.

00:36:20.500 --> 00:36:22.750
Exterior side
street, continuous.

00:36:22.750 --> 00:36:25.450
Del notices the new route.

00:36:25.450 --> 00:36:27.290
What are you doing?

00:36:27.290 --> 00:36:28.830
Shortcut.

00:36:28.830 --> 00:36:30.430
Newspapers swirl in the wind.

00:36:30.430 --> 00:36:31.720
Del backs them away.

00:36:31.720 --> 00:36:33.340
Just ahead, they
pass a homeless woman

00:36:33.340 --> 00:36:35.170
wrangling the storm
of loose newspapers

00:36:35.170 --> 00:36:38.730
back into her plastic bags.

00:36:38.730 --> 00:36:40.100
Sonya?

00:36:40.100 --> 00:36:44.020
She looks up to see a brown
cow flash across the sidewalk

00:36:44.020 --> 00:36:46.730
and disappear into
a nearby alley.

00:36:46.730 --> 00:36:49.130
Del and Sonya skid to a stop.

00:36:49.130 --> 00:36:50.510
What?

00:36:50.510 --> 00:36:53.840
They turn to see Ralph, 37, a
heavy butcher sprinting around

00:36:53.840 --> 00:36:56.360
the corner in white work
clothes splattered with blood.

00:36:56.360 --> 00:36:58.010
He stops, sweaty
and out of breath.

00:36:58.010 --> 00:37:00.110
His beard curls up,
giving him the appearance

00:37:00.110 --> 00:37:02.010
of the perfect villain.

00:37:02.010 --> 00:37:05.498
You didn't have to see a
cow come this way, did you?

00:37:05.498 --> 00:37:06.740
Um.

00:37:06.740 --> 00:37:07.850
No.

00:37:07.850 --> 00:37:09.050
Ralph eyes Del.

00:37:09.050 --> 00:37:12.320
Del calmly folds his
hands in his lap.

00:37:12.320 --> 00:37:14.130
You sure about that?

00:37:14.130 --> 00:37:15.600
A cow?

00:37:15.600 --> 00:37:17.840
Yes.

00:37:17.840 --> 00:37:20.920
I don't think we'd miss
something like that.

00:37:20.920 --> 00:37:21.530
A cow?

00:37:21.530 --> 00:37:23.120
Right, Sonya?

00:37:23.120 --> 00:37:25.080
A cow?

00:37:25.080 --> 00:37:26.960
Right.

00:37:26.960 --> 00:37:28.500
Right.

00:37:28.500 --> 00:37:30.300
Ralph studies Sonya.

00:37:30.300 --> 00:37:32.430
She's clearly hiding something.

00:37:32.430 --> 00:37:34.320
They stare each other
down and it takes

00:37:34.320 --> 00:37:37.020
everything she has to hold on.

00:37:37.020 --> 00:37:39.390
He steps a step
closer toward her,

00:37:39.390 --> 00:37:41.790
entering her force
field of pain.

00:37:41.790 --> 00:37:45.150
Sadness wafts off
like a bad smell.

00:37:45.150 --> 00:37:50.430
She closes her eyes and shakes
her head teetering on the edge.

00:37:50.430 --> 00:37:52.600
Ralph looks to Del.

00:37:52.600 --> 00:37:54.587
She just broke up
with her boyfriend.

00:37:54.587 --> 00:37:55.900
Dad.

00:37:55.900 --> 00:37:57.860
OK, boss.

00:37:57.860 --> 00:37:58.980
OK.

00:37:58.980 --> 00:38:00.870
Ralph looks left,
looks right, and takes

00:38:00.870 --> 00:38:02.280
off as fast as he can.

00:38:02.280 --> 00:38:04.860
Del waits for him to clear
the corner then looks down

00:38:04.860 --> 00:38:05.740
the alley.

00:38:05.740 --> 00:38:07.830
A trashcan rattles.

00:38:07.830 --> 00:38:10.980
Del rises and walks
stiffly toward the sound.

00:38:10.980 --> 00:38:13.026
Sonya starts to fold
up the wheelchair.

00:38:13.026 --> 00:38:15.160
Leave it.

00:38:15.160 --> 00:38:17.950
She parks the chair at
the edge of the alley.

00:38:17.950 --> 00:38:19.710
Exterior alley, continuous.

00:38:19.710 --> 00:38:22.510
The cow rummages
through bags of trash.

00:38:22.510 --> 00:38:26.080
Sensing Del and Sonya, it
pops its head up and freezes.

00:38:26.080 --> 00:38:28.670
Del inches closer.

00:38:28.670 --> 00:38:30.232
Should we?

00:38:30.232 --> 00:38:31.510
Shhh...

00:38:31.510 --> 00:38:33.760
Words have no place here.

00:38:33.760 --> 00:38:36.880
Del and the cow
stare eye to eye.

00:38:36.880 --> 00:38:39.430
He takes his hat
off in reverence.

00:38:39.430 --> 00:38:41.480
The cow blinks at Del.

00:38:41.480 --> 00:38:43.210
Hi, friend.

00:38:43.210 --> 00:38:45.600
Del smiles softly.

00:38:45.600 --> 00:38:47.530
Hi, friend.

00:38:47.530 --> 00:38:49.070
The two share a breath.

00:38:49.070 --> 00:38:52.650
The cow's nostrils
are soft and moist.

00:38:52.650 --> 00:38:53.990
And then...

00:38:53.990 --> 00:38:57.990
It jets, trotting
away like a unicorn.

00:38:57.990 --> 00:39:00.710
Del limps a couple
of steps after it.

00:39:00.710 --> 00:39:03.060
Don't let them
take you, Comrade!

00:39:03.060 --> 00:39:05.220
The cow gallops around a corner.

00:39:05.220 --> 00:39:07.250
Resist!

00:39:07.250 --> 00:39:09.060
Del looks back to Sonya.

00:39:09.060 --> 00:39:12.180
His eyes are lit up with magic.

00:39:12.180 --> 00:39:15.547
Do you think it'll make it?

00:39:15.547 --> 00:39:16.380
Anything's possible.

00:39:20.170 --> 00:39:22.900
With that, our event
draws to a close.

00:39:22.900 --> 00:39:25.900
Made possible by the awesome
creative contributions

00:39:25.900 --> 00:39:29.410
of Burlee and Abel Vang
and the distinctive voices

00:39:29.410 --> 00:39:30.460
of Lou Diamond Phillips.

00:39:30.460 --> 00:39:31.420
Stay safe and healthy.

00:39:31.420 --> 00:39:35.140
Stephanie Beatriz, Michael
Pena, and Taylor Russell,

00:39:35.140 --> 00:39:37.540
and the Academy
Nicholl Fellowships

00:39:37.540 --> 00:39:39.130
in Screenwriting team.

00:39:39.130 --> 00:39:41.980
We also shout out a special
note of love and support

00:39:41.980 --> 00:39:44.740
to the Nicholl Fellowships in
Screenwriting Committee whose

00:39:44.740 --> 00:39:48.430
leadership and energy guided
the 2020 screenwriting

00:39:48.430 --> 00:39:52.150
competition to this moment of
culmination and celebration.

00:39:52.150 --> 00:39:55.990
And now to all of you who tuned
in and got involved today.

00:39:55.990 --> 00:39:59.080
As we get ready to hit fade
out on our time together,

00:39:59.080 --> 00:40:01.120
I've got a question for you.

00:40:01.120 --> 00:40:03.770
How have Vanar,
Kate, Jane, James,

00:40:03.770 --> 00:40:05.980
and Beth's story spoken to you?

00:40:05.980 --> 00:40:07.270
Are you inspired?

00:40:07.270 --> 00:40:08.910
More determined than ever?

00:40:08.910 --> 00:40:11.170
Do you see yourself
in their dedication?

00:40:11.170 --> 00:40:13.660
Do you see yourself in
their stories, characters,

00:40:13.660 --> 00:40:14.620
and conflicts?

00:40:14.620 --> 00:40:17.020
Surprises and revelations?

00:40:17.020 --> 00:40:20.550
Do they help you
feel seen and heard?

00:40:20.550 --> 00:40:21.830
We hope so.

00:40:21.830 --> 00:40:24.380
We hope to hear your
story very, very soon.

00:40:24.380 --> 00:40:26.990
And maybe this time
next year, we'll

00:40:26.990 --> 00:40:29.150
be celebrating your story.

00:40:29.150 --> 00:40:31.630
To get started, please
visit oscars.org.

00:40:31.630 --> 00:40:35.810
Thank you, take
care, and stay safe.

00:40:35.810 --> 00:40:37.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

